  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nIt's been an active week of thunderstorms across Southern New\nMexico. Upper high fixed over the Colorado River promoted north\nflow, pushing storms off the mountains and down into the lowlands\nquickly. Sufficient moisture added to storm coverage and flood\nrisk. However, it looks like that pattern will change this\nweekend, pumping the breaks on rain chances over the next several\ndays.\n\nUpper high has made a slight eastward shift over the Four Corners\npast few days, and will linger there through at least next \nTuesday. This will push the primary plume of monsoonal moisture \nfurther west again toward Arizona. Drier fetch of air moving up \nthe Rio Grande at lower levels will also support a drying trend. \nPrecipitable water values around 1.0\" today will fall to 0.8-0.9\" \nby Sunday. While this doesn't seem like much, it will greatly \nreduce the chance for early afternoon initiation over the high \nterrain and subsequently eliminate outflow forcing during the \nlate afternoon and evening hours. A few isolated (5-10% coverage) \nshowers will still be possible on Saturday/Sunday, but coverage \nwill be greatly reduced. No major changes to temperatures, with \nlowland highs in the upper 90s.\n\nRain chances will be limited mostly to the Gila and Western NM\nMonday/Tuesday, with scattered (20-40% coverage) storms each\nafternoon. Storms will be capable of gusty winds and localized\nflooding. Elsewhere, continued dry conditions with temperatures\nwarming back toward 100-104 degrees along the Rio Grande Valley \nand Tularosa Basin.\n\nUpper high moves east during the latter half of next week, finally\nbeginning to look like a classic monsoon pattern. Rain chances\nwill expand area wide again by next Wednesday. Precipitable water\nvalues 1.2-1.4\" will pose a risk for flash flooding.",
